<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-music/">Apple Music</a> subscribers can now stream directly to compatible Bluesound speakers and hi-fi equipment, allowing playback without sending audio via <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/airplay/">AirPlay</a> 2.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/apple-music-new-logo-red.jpg" alt="" width="1672" height="941" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1068878" /><br/>
Bluesound offers a range of high-end home audio products ranging from wireless speakers and soundbars to streaming components that connect to existing hi-fi systems. Its products use BluOS, a platform that lets users control music and link audio equipment across multiple rooms.<br/>
<br/>
The <a href="https://www.bluesound.com/blogs/news/apple-music-now-available-on-bluesound">new integration</a> makes &zwnj;Apple Music&zwnj; accessible within the BluOS Controller app. Once a subscriber signs in, the player retrieves the music itself, freeing their iPhone or <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a> from handling the audio stream. &zwnj;AirPlay&zwnj; 2 remains available.<br/>
<br/>
For households with several BluOS products, the addition also brings &zwnj;Apple Music&zwnj; into the same system used to manage playback around the home. Users can play music across grouped devices or choose different music for individual rooms.<br/>
<br/>
